+# Daily Grind rewards
+
+The homepage includes a native rewards registration form backed by `POST /api/verify-email`. It uses the browser Email Verification API and Email Verification Protocol (EVP), rather than sending a code or magic link.
+
+## Configuration
+
+Requires **Node.js 24+**. Install dependencies with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and set:
+
+- `APP_ORIGIN`: the exact deployed HTTPS origin, without a path or trailing slash. This is the trusted key-binding audience and accepted POST origin; it is never inferred from proxy headers.
+- `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN`: a valid Chrome Email Verification origin-trial token registered for that origin. The server supplies it in the `Origin-Trial` response header. Register through the [Chrome relying-party setup instructions](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification/verifier). A token is specific to the deployed origin and cannot be supplied in this repository.
+- `DATABASE_PATH`: SQLite database path (defaults to `./data/rewards.sqlite`). Put this on a persistent writable volume and include it in backups. Accounts contain a generated member ID, name, original email, unique case-insensitive email key, and verification timestamp.
+- `PORT`: defaults to `11222`.
+
+`pnpm start` starts the Node application; static-only hosting cannot supply server nonces or verify registrations. Terminate HTTPS at your hosting platform/reverse proxy and forward requests to Node. The default localhost HTTP origin is only for local development; successful browser EVP verification requires a supported secure deployment, browser, and participating email provider. No server was started during implementation.
+
+## Registration behavior
+
+1. `GET /` renders a fresh 256-bit nonce in the hidden `autocomplete="email-verification-token"` input alongside the email input. The expected nonce remains in SQLite, keyed by an opaque HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax cookie. Responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`; a restored back/forward page reloads for a new nonce.
+2. Native form submission lets the browser supply its EVT and key-binding JWT. The server atomically consumes the nonce before processing the attempt. Challenges expire after ten minutes; every rendered retry has a fresh challenge.
+3. The server checks DNS TXT delegation, HTTPS discovery metadata, provider signatures, required expiry and issue time, verified email, browser holder signature, nonce, origin audience, and presentation digest. Verification uses `@sd-jwt/core` and `jose`. Issuers omitting `kid` are supported by trying the matching public keys. Discovery requests reject private network addresses and redirects, pin DNS results, and bound response sizes and request duration.
+4. Only after all checks pass does the server persist the rewards account. Duplicate email addresses do not create another account or overwrite the original member record. This is membership registration; it does not establish a login session or implement the existing `/account` destination.
+
+If the browser/provider cannot supply a token, permission is declined, or verification fails, registration remains incomplete and the form explains how to retry. **No OTP, magic-link, or fallback verification email is sent.** This deliberately overrides the guidance's email fallback to satisfy the requested no-email flow. Account creation requires successful ownership verification; unsupported browsers are never treated as verified.
+
+Opening multiple sign-up tabs replaces the session challenge cookie; an older tab will ask the customer to retry. The in-process POST throttle allows twenty attempts per minute per socket IP; configure your hosting platform's rate limits for larger deployments or shared proxies. The SQLite store supports a deployment with persistent local disk, not independent ephemeral replicas.
+
+The browser API is an origin-trial feature. Real-world completion depends on current browser/provider participation and a valid trial token. See the [Chrome Email Verification documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification) for prerequisites. Verification proves account ownership, not inbox deliverability.
+
+## Validation
+
+- `pnpm run build`: syntax checks, including the server entrypoint without importing or starting it.
+- `pnpm test`: offline tests of the pure request handler, persistent store, and cryptographic verifier. They use locally generated signing keys and injected issuer/DNS fixtures. They do not import `server.mjs`, open a listener, or perform browser verification.
+
+The existing Unsplash coffee photograph is reused. Fonts load from Google Fonts with local fallbacks.
